'' The last token a macro expansion emits and the next token of the enclosing stream
'' re-lex as ONE when nothing separates them in the source. Every case, and the
'' measurements behind it, are in the source's own header.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/pp/src/macro-adjacency.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered

'' The fold from tests/vendor/fbc-tests/pp/hello.bas: twelve one-character literals,
'' and the empty string that 'letter' + '__' turns into. Without the join the chain
'' ends on a bare 'letter' instead.
'' EXPECT-OUT: global str $str8 "!"
'' EXPECT-OUT: global str $str9 ""

'' A NUMBER meeting a number: 'TAIL( 12 )34' is one literal, folded here.
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 1234

'' An OPERATOR meeting an operator: '<' then '>' is '<>', so this is a comparison
'' and not the syntax error two separate tokens would be.
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.ne

'' An ARGUMENT's tail meeting an identifier: 'TAIL( ab )cd' names abcd, not ab.
'' EXPECT-OUT: load @abcd

'' The join happens BEFORE the rescan looks for macro names, so 'N' never expands
'' and the name is Nab.
'' EXPECT-OUT: load @nab

'' The JOINED name is itself a function-like macro and takes its argument from the
'' source that followed the join: 'TAILFOO( 0 )bar( 3 )' is '(3 * 2)'.
'' EXPECT-OUT: mul

'' 'BEE' is joined away rather than invoked: qBEE, and no 999 anywhere.
'' EXPECT-OUT: load @qbee
'' EXPECT-NOT: const i64 999
